<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=bronze">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Tools and materials</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bronze</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as bronze industry</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bronze is an alloy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>, and the term is also used loosely for related copper alloys such as brass. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> it was the principal metal for weapons, tools and prestige goods in empires such as the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>. Much later, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a> became famous for its cast copper-alloy sculptures, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bronze.jpg" alt="Bronze" width="320" height="357" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Metropolitan Museum of Art · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bull%27s_head_ornament_for_a_lyre_MET_DP260070.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Hittites held a strategically important position astride the routes connecting Mesopotamia to the Aegean, and merchants working under Hittite protection took part in the long-distance tin and copper trade that supplied the ancient world's <b>bronze industry</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Bronze</b> spearheads and ritual spears are among the finds at the capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-zimbabwe">Kingdom of Zimbabwe · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Bronze</b> chandeliers hung in the mosques, the one at Taza of 1294 weighing three tons and holding 514 lamps.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marinid-sultanate">Marinid Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The tell of the ancient city, Sidon IV, lies beneath the ruined Castle of St Louis, with an unbroken sequence from Early <b>Bronze</b> I to III on bedrock from about 3200 BCE and a modest third-millennium settlement of houses and tombs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Bronze</b>, jade and pottery production was concentrated in workshops near the capitals, such as the large casting site at Zhouyuan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Bronze</b> finds include bracelets, rings, anklets, wires and rods, spearheads, axes and adzes, hooks, blades and small bells.</blockquote><a href="/culture/ban-chiang-culture">Ban Chiang culture · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li></ul></section>
